|~ qlang operand catalog — codeAsData family. The ring that
   closes source text → quote → pipeValue without leaving the
   language. `parse` flips a string and a quote, the vector of
   steps a `~(…)` literal makes; `apply code` runs the quote its
   captured arg answers against the subject, subject first like
   every other operand. Trail-emitted suffix quotes flow through
   pipeValue, so `error !| /trail | as :t | start | apply t`
   re-runs deflected steps against a fresh subject. ~|

:parse
  |~~ Flips a string and a quote, the way keyword flips a
      string and a keyword: source text reads as the quote of
      its steps, and a quote prints back as its text. Pair with
      `apply /` to evaluate source text without leaving qlang.
      Parse errors from the underlying peggy parser lift onto
      the fail-track as ordinary error values, so malformed
      sources surface through !| like every other runtime
      failure.

      ~("1 | add 1" | parse | count | eq 2)
      ~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))
      ~(~(add 1) | parse | eq "add 1")
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/parse
            :category :codeAsData
            :subject [:string :quote]
            :returns [:quote :string]}

::ParseSubjectNotStringOrQuoteError
  |~~ `parse` requires a String or Quote subject.

      ~(
        42 | parse
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ParseSubjectNotStringOrQuoteError :number]
       )
  ~~|

:apply
  |~~ Runs the quote its captured arg answers against the
      subject, under the fork rule: the declarations the code
      makes stay inside it, and only its value comes out. The
      subject comes first, as for every other operand, so
      `x | apply /` runs a quote held as the subject against
      itself, and `"src" | parse | apply /` evaluates source
      text.

      The quote's first step rides `|` against the subject
      unless the quote carries a leading combinator (`*`, `!|`),
      which routes it through that combinator. A pipeline-suffix
      shape — exactly the form `:trail` records surface — is
      therefore directly re-executable.

      ~(42 | apply ~(type | eq :null) | eq false)
      ~([1 2 3] | apply ~(* mul 2) | eq [2 4 6])
      ~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])
      ~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply / | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/apply
            :category :codeAsData
            :subject :any
            :returns :any
            :modifiers [:quote]}


::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ `apply` requires its captured arg to answer a Quote — the
      code it runs against the subject.

      ~(
        42 | apply 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ApplyCodeNotQuoteError :number]
       )
  ~~|
